WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Speaker 1: Let's go. So many&nbsp;
different folks wanting to chat today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.480
Let's go next to Glenn from&nbsp;
Virginia. Glenn, you're on the air.&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.280
Speaker 4: David, can you hear me?
Speaker 1: I can hear you just fine.&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.080
Speaker 4: OK, great, this is my first time&nbsp;
calling. Thanks for taking the call. Pleasure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.720
Mike. My question for you is with respect to&nbsp;
voting rights, just to give you context. I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:32.720
an African-American in my mid-forties and I fear&nbsp;
that there will likely be no meaningful voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:40.400
rights legislation coming down the pipeline. So&nbsp;
my question for you is, do you think the Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.480
mischaracterized? Well, let me rephrase.&nbsp;
Instead of saying, mischaracterize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.560
do you think that they did a poor job&nbsp;
in messaging as you know so much with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.520
respect to legislation comes down to&nbsp;
messaging? And what I mean by that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.800
is with voting rights legislation, do you&nbsp;
think the Democrats would have done themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:08.640
more of a service if they sold it as an issue&nbsp;
for the less powerful, the less connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:18.960
those who are in perhaps rural areas and having&nbsp;
access to voting as opposed to selling it more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.440
a race issue with respect to black and brown&nbsp;
people. Do you think the Democrats sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.440
fumbled the football, so to speak, in that regard?
Speaker 1: It's always hard to go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.160
and say what what might have been, but it's a&nbsp;
good question, which is if the framing along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.800
defending voting rights and pushing back against&nbsp;
all of these wacky right wing bills had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.040
they are going after the little guy rather than&nbsp;
they are targeting disproportionately nonwhite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.720
minorities. Would it have been better received?
Speaker 2: Here's the thing&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.840
Speaker 1: for the people on the left&nbsp;
who understand the issue, they're already&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.440
Speaker 2: behind supporting
Speaker 1: voting rights in a real way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.789
and fighting back against what Republicans
Speaker 2: are doing. And if the&nbsp;

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:05.200
Speaker 1: left had framed it in the way&nbsp;
you suggest the right is still going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.520
just as against it, and they would come up&nbsp;
with some way to justify it. And so then it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.000
just becomes a fight of like, do they do&nbsp;
it effectively or not? I just don't know.&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.080
Speaker 2: They're sometimes one of the prop.
Speaker 1: One of the mistakes we make is we say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:22.560
Hey, you know what? Here's the talking point they&nbsp;
came up with. And that talking point was so easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.880
defeated. Here's a different talking point that&nbsp;
would have been much better. But what you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.520
to remember is if the talking point had been&nbsp;
different, their response also would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.240
different. And so I just don't know, because&nbsp;
fundamentally Republican elected officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.840
know that they need all of these different&nbsp;
voter suppression tactics in order to win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.320
and they were going to justify them one way&nbsp;
or the other. So it's hard for me to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.840
that it would have been much more successfully
Speaker 2: argued&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.000
Speaker 1: if they argued along class&nbsp;
rather than race lines. And to be honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.480
they did also argue it along class lines&nbsp;
because often it's about poor areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.920
not nonwhite areas.
Speaker 4:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:09.680
Yeah, I agree. I just look back at some of the&nbsp;
tactics of the Obama administration, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:17.840
in the 2012 campaign. They sort of anticipated&nbsp;
the backlash, so to speak, and they were much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:25.600
nimble and making the class warfare argument.&nbsp;
And even if the Republican attempts are based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:33.200
racial sensitivities, I just think you&nbsp;
do yourself a lot more of a service if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.640
you make the class warfare argument.
Speaker 1: Yeah, I'm not disagreeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.589
with you. My only caveat
Speaker 2: would be, I think the&nbsp;

00:03:39.589 --> 00:03:46.080
Speaker 1: Republican Party would have been ready&nbsp;
for that as well, but I don't think you're wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.400
OK, fair enough. Thanks for my call. All&nbsp;
right, thanks, Glenn. There is Glenn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.040
Very, very important topic. I mean, a&nbsp;
great great great question to be asking.

